  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If the speed of an airplane is measured in $\\text{mi/hour},$ what are the units of its acceleration?",
  "worked_solution": "We can find the acceleration of an object by computing the slope of its speed-time graph. So, let's draw an example of a speed-time graph where the speed is measured in $\\text{mi/hour}$ and the time is measured in $\\text{hours}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/10e3d32b8d1fc512f6ced10dacc5b1c3df2dc1e7564c739a459b8661c63c68c6.png)\n\nIn this case, the slope is computed as\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{\\text{change in speed}}{\\text{change in time}},\n$$\n\nand so the units of the slope are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{units of speed}}{\\text{units of time}}=\\frac{\\text{mi/hour}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the units of acceleration are $\\frac{\\text{mi}}{{\\text{hour}}^{2}}.$",
